1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,501
In lesson one, you saw that a
conversation is a list you resend every time, and that

2
00:00:06,501 --> 00:00:07,635
it keeps growing.

3
00:00:07,985 --> 00:00:13,549
In this lesson, you'll measure that
growth, learn what the context window is, and

4
00:00:13,549 --> 00:00:18,976
keep the conversation small with a
sliding window, without breaking tool calls.

5
00:00:20,776 --> 00:00:23,881
You'll explain tokens,
and the context window.

6
00:00:24,481 --> 00:00:27,868
Measure how a
conversation grows, turn by turn.

7
00:00:28,468 --> 00:00:32,014
Keep only the last few
turns, with a sliding window.

8
00:00:32,614 --> 00:00:36,746
And cut only at turn
boundaries, so tool calls stay valid.

9
00:00:38,546 --> 00:00:39,686
First, a picture.

10
00:00:40,036 --> 00:00:43,322
A classroom whiteboard
holds only so much writing.

11
00:00:43,672 --> 00:00:48,401
When it's full, the teacher must
erase something before writing anything new.

12
00:00:48,751 --> 00:00:51,854
The context window is
the model's whiteboard.

13
00:00:52,204 --> 00:00:58,137
It's everything the model can read in one
request: the system prompt, the tools, the

14
00:00:58,137 --> 00:01:00,467
messages, and room for its reply.

15
00:01:01,067 --> 00:01:02,725
It's measured in tokens.

16
00:01:03,075 --> 00:01:06,549
A token is a piece of
text, a few characters long.

17
00:01:06,899 --> 00:01:09,691
In English, roughly
three quarters of a word.

18
00:01:10,291 --> 00:01:14,364
The window size is the most
tokens one request can hold.

19
00:01:14,964 --> 00:01:18,897
And you pay for every input
token you send, on every call.

20
00:01:19,497 --> 00:01:25,565
Today's Claude models have very large
context windows, hundreds of thousands of

21
00:01:25,565 --> 00:01:26,103
tokens.

22
00:01:26,453 --> 00:01:27,593
So why worry?

23
00:01:27,943 --> 00:01:33,631
Because long before you hit the limit,
a growing conversation costs more on every

24
00:01:33,631 --> 00:01:36,019
turn, and every reply gets slower.

25
00:01:37,819 --> 00:01:38,894
Let's measure it.

26
00:01:39,244 --> 00:01:42,775
The code is on the left,
and its output is on the right.

27
00:01:43,375 --> 00:01:45,866
We build a conversation of five turns.

28
00:01:46,216 --> 00:01:50,929
Each turn is a short question,
and a long answer, like real tutoring.

29
00:01:51,529 --> 00:01:53,677
After each turn we print the size.

30
00:01:54,027 --> 00:01:57,307
About a hundred and
fifty tokens more every turn.

31
00:01:57,657 --> 00:02:00,742
And remember, the whole
thing is sent on every call.

32
00:02:01,092 --> 00:02:03,149
That's the cost of perfect memory.

33
00:02:03,749 --> 00:02:06,700
Now the simplest fix: a sliding window.

34
00:02:07,050 --> 00:02:09,221
We keep only the last two turns.

35
00:02:09,821 --> 00:02:14,415
Four messages, about two hundred
and ninety tokens, less than half.

36
00:02:14,765 --> 00:02:17,947
The oldest message kept is: explain loops.

37
00:02:18,547 --> 00:02:19,915
But here's the price.

38
00:02:20,265 --> 00:02:21,908
We ask: what's my name?

39
00:02:22,258 --> 00:02:24,315
And the answer is: I don't know.

40
00:02:24,665 --> 00:02:28,719
The name was in turn one, and
turn one slid out of the window.

41
00:02:30,519 --> 00:02:33,124
There are two common
ways to size a window.

42
00:02:33,474 --> 00:02:36,056
By turns: keep the last N turns.

43
00:02:36,406 --> 00:02:38,667
It's simple, and easy to reason about.

44
00:02:39,017 --> 00:02:42,485
But one turn with a huge
answer can still be too big.

45
00:02:43,085 --> 00:02:47,758
Or by tokens: keep as many
recent turns as fit a token budget.

46
00:02:48,108 --> 00:02:50,409
That keeps the size under a known limit.

47
00:02:50,759 --> 00:02:57,158
To count tokens you can estimate, about
four characters per token, or ask the API

48
00:02:57,158 --> 00:03:00,319
exactly, with its count tokens endpoint.

49
00:03:02,119 --> 00:03:03,851
Here's the project's version.

50
00:03:04,201 --> 00:03:08,598
First, a rough token estimate:
about four characters per token.

51
00:03:08,948 --> 00:03:11,063
Good enough to decide when to trim.

52
00:03:11,413 --> 00:03:15,641
For exact numbers, use the
API's count tokens endpoint.

53
00:03:16,241 --> 00:03:18,341
Now, where do developers go wrong?

54
00:03:18,691 --> 00:03:20,187
Cutting in the wrong place.

55
00:03:20,537 --> 00:03:24,544
A turn starts with a user
message whose content is plain text.

56
00:03:24,894 --> 00:03:30,236
Tool results are also sent with the user
role, but they're a list of blocks, so they

57
00:03:30,236 --> 00:03:31,444
don't start a turn.

58
00:03:33,244 --> 00:03:38,589
The conversation class holds the
messages, and a summary we'll use next lesson.

59
00:03:38,939 --> 00:03:41,236
Its size is the estimate of both.

60
00:03:41,836 --> 00:03:44,995
This method finds the
index where each turn starts.

61
00:03:45,595 --> 00:03:49,271
And the window starts at the
turn start, so many turns back.

62
00:03:49,621 --> 00:03:54,720
That guarantees a tool use block
and its tool result are never separated.

63
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:59,175
Let's dry run it on a
chat with a tool call.

64
00:03:59,525 --> 00:04:03,371
Message zero, the user's
own words: a turn starts here.

65
00:04:03,971 --> 00:04:07,025
Message one: the
assistant asks for a tool.

66
00:04:07,625 --> 00:04:09,679
Message two: the tool result.

67
00:04:10,029 --> 00:04:12,307
User role, but not a turn start.

68
00:04:12,907 --> 00:04:14,731
Message three: the reply.

69
00:04:15,331 --> 00:04:17,258
Message four starts turn two.

70
00:04:17,608 --> 00:04:20,285
With keep turns two,
the window starts here.

71
00:04:20,885 --> 00:04:22,840
And everything after it is kept.

72
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:25,804
Pause and try it.

73
00:04:26,154 --> 00:04:27,782
Here are eight messages.

74
00:04:28,132 --> 00:04:32,705
Message three is a tool use,
and message four is its tool result.

75
00:04:33,305 --> 00:04:36,461
Which messages does a
window of two turns keep?

76
00:04:43,061 --> 00:04:45,882
Turns start at messages zero, two and six.

77
00:04:46,232 --> 00:04:52,006
Keeping two turns means starting at
message two, so it keeps messages two to seven,

78
00:04:52,006 --> 00:04:54,718
and the tool use stays with its result.

79
00:04:56,518 --> 00:04:57,660
Interview corner.

80
00:04:58,010 --> 00:05:02,367
What is a context window, and
how do you manage it in a long chat?

81
00:05:04,967 --> 00:05:11,063
A good answer: it's the most tokens a
model can read in one request, including the

82
00:05:11,063 --> 00:05:14,409
system prompt, tools,
messages and the reply.

83
00:05:14,759 --> 00:05:20,524
You manage it with a sliding window of
recent turns, summaries of older turns, and

84
00:05:20,524 --> 00:05:23,548
long-term facts that
you fetch when needed.

85
00:05:23,898 --> 00:05:27,897
The trap: saying a big window
means you never need to manage it.

86
00:05:28,247 --> 00:05:31,328
Cost and latency grow
long before the limit.

87
00:05:31,928 --> 00:05:32,865
Follow-up.

88
00:05:33,215 --> 00:05:35,846
Why can't you just
drop the oldest message?

89
00:05:38,446 --> 00:05:40,774
Because messages come in linked groups.

90
00:05:41,124 --> 00:05:46,897
Dropping a tool use but keeping its tool
result, or the reverse, makes the request

91
00:05:46,897 --> 00:05:47,460
invalid.

92
00:05:47,810 --> 00:05:49,666
So you cut at turn boundaries.

93
00:05:50,016 --> 00:05:55,427
The trap is forgetting that tool results
are sent with the user role, so not every

94
00:05:55,427 --> 00:05:57,209
user message starts a turn.

95
00:05:59,009 --> 00:05:59,868
Quick check.

96
00:06:00,218 --> 00:06:03,060
A sliding window
keeps the last three turns.

97
00:06:03,410 --> 00:06:04,724
What's the main risk?

98
00:06:10,924 --> 00:06:14,493
Facts from early turns,
like the user's name, are lost.

99
00:06:14,843 --> 00:06:17,295
The window keeps costs down, but forgets.

100
00:06:19,095 --> 00:06:20,185
Let's recap.

101
00:06:20,535 --> 00:06:25,908
The context window is everything one
request can hold, measured in tokens.

102
00:06:26,508 --> 00:06:30,599
A conversation grows every
turn, which costs money and time.

103
00:06:31,199 --> 00:06:36,569
A sliding window keeps the last few
turns, or as many as fit a token budget.

104
00:06:37,169 --> 00:06:41,227
And always cut where a turn
starts, so tool pairs stay together.

105
00:06:41,827 --> 00:06:43,840
Next lesson: summary memory.

106
00:06:44,190 --> 00:06:49,624
Instead of throwing old turns away,
we'll fold them into a short summary, and get

107
00:06:49,624 --> 00:06:50,563
the name back.
